      "text": "To find \\(4^4 \\cdot 5^4\\), we can use the property of exponents that states \\((a \\cdot b)^n = a^n \\cdot b^n\\). Here, we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n4^4 \\cdot 5^4 = (4 \\cdot 5)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the product inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 = 20\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n(4 \\cdot 5)^4 = 20^4\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\(20^4\\). We can do this step-by-step:\n\nFirst, calculate \\(20^2\\):\n\n\\[\n20^2 = 20 \\cdot 20 = 400\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(20^4\\) by squaring \\(20^2\\):\n\n\\[\n20^4 = (20^2)^2 = 400^2\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate \\(400^2\\):\n\n\\[\n400^2 = 400 \\cdot 400 = 160000\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(4^4 \\cdot 5^4\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{160000}\n\\]\n\nGiven the function $f(x)= \\begin{cases} x\\sin \\frac {πx}{2},-2 < x\\leqslant 0 \\\\ 2^{- \\frac {x}{2}},0 < x\\leqslant 2\\end{cases}$, find the value of $ \\int_{ -2 }^{ 2 }f(x)\\,dx$.\n\nTo evaluate the integral \\(\\int_{-2}^2 f(x) \\, dx\\) for the given piecewise function \\(f(x) = \\begin{cases} x \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2}, & -2 < x \\leq 0 \\\\ 2^{- \\frac{x}{2}}, & 0 < x \\leq 2 \\end{cases}\\), we can break the integral into two parts corresponding to the intervals where the function is defined differently.\n\nFirst, consider the integral from \\(-2\\) to \\(0\\):\n\\[\n\\int_{-2}^0 x \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx.\n\\]\nWe use integration by parts, where we let \\(u = x\\) and \\(dv = \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx\\). Then, \\(du = dx\\) and \\(v = -\\frac{2}{\\pi} \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\). Applying the integration by parts formula \\(\\int u \\, dv = uv - \\int v \\, du\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\int_{-2}^0 x \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx = \\left[ -\\frac{2x}{\\pi} \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\right]_{-2}^0 - \\int_{-2}^0 -\\frac{2}{\\pi} \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx.\n\\]\nEvaluating the first term:\n\\[\n\\left[ -\\frac{2x}{\\pi} \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\right]_{-2}^0 = 0 - \\left( -\\frac{2(-2)}{\\pi} \\cos \\left( -\\frac{\\pi(-2)}{2} \\right) \\right) = 0 + \\frac{4}{\\pi} \\cos \\pi = \\frac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot (-1) = -\\frac{4}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nFor the second term, we have:\n\\[\n\\int_{-2}^0 \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{-2}^0 \\cos \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx.\n\\]\nWe use the substitution \\(w = \\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\), so \\(dw = \\frac{\\pi}{2} dx\\) and \\(dx = \\frac{2}{\\pi} dw\\). When \\(x = -2\\), \\(w = -\\pi\\), and when \\(x = 0\\), \\(w = 0\\). Thus, the integral becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{\\pi} \\int_{-\\pi}^0 \\cos w \\cdot \\frac{2}{\\pi} \\, dw = \\frac{4}{\\pi^2} \\int_{-\\pi}^0 \\cos w \\, dw = \\frac{4}{\\pi^2} \\left[ \\sin w \\right]_{-\\pi}^0 = \\frac{4}{\\pi^2} (\\sin 0 - \\sin(-\\pi)) = \\frac{4}{\\pi^2} (0 - 0) = 0.\n\\]\nTherefore, the integral from \\(-2\\) to \\(0\\) is:\n\\[\n\\int_{-2}^0 x \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx = -\\frac{4}{\\pi}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the integral from \\(0\\) to \\(2\\):\n\\[\n\\int_0^2 2^{- \\frac{x}{2}} \\, dx.\n\\]\nWe use the substitution \\(u = -\\frac{x}{2}\\), so \\(du = -\\frac{1}{2} dx\\) and \\(dx = -2 \\, du\\). When \\(x = 0\\), \\(u = 0\\), and when \\(x = 2\\), \\(u = -1\\). Thus, the integral becomes:\n\\[\n\\int_0^2 2^{- \\frac{x}{2}} \\, dx = \\int_0^{-1} 2^u \\cdot (-2) \\, du = -2 \\int_0^{-1} 2^u \\, du = 2 \\int_{-1}^0 2^u \\, du.\n\\]\nWe use the substitution \\(v = 2^u\\), so \\(dv = 2^u \\ln 2 \\, du\\) and \\(du = \\frac{1}{v \\ln 2} dv\\). When \\(u = 0\\), \\(v = 1\\), and when \\(u = -1\\), \\(v = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Thus, the integral becomes:\n\\[\n2 \\int_{-1}^0 2^u \\, du = 2 \\int_1^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{v \\ln 2} \\, dv = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} \\int_1^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{v} \\, dv = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} \\left[ \\ln v \\right]_1^{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} \\left( \\ln \\frac{1}{2} - \\ln 1 \\right) = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} \\left( \\ln \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} \\ln \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{\\ln 2} (-\\ln 2) = -2.\n\\]\nTherefore, the integral from \\(0\\) to \\(2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\int_0^2 2^{- \\frac{x}{2}} \\, dx = -2.\n\\]\n\nAdding the two integrals together, we get:\n\\[\n\\int_{-2}^2 f(x) \\, dx = \\int_{-2}^0 x \\sin \\frac{\\pi x}{2} \\, dx + \\int_0^2 2^{- \\frac{x}{2}} \\, dx = -\\frac{4}{\\pi} - 2 = -\\frac{4}{\\pi} - \\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi} = -\\frac{4 + 2\\pi}{\\pi} = -\\frac{2(2 + \\pi)}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThus, the value of the integral is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{2(2 + \\pi)}{\\pi}}.\n\\]\n\nPoints \\(A, B, C, D, E, F\\) are positioned on a line in that order. If \\(AC = 15\\), \\(CE = 21\\), \\(CF = 27\\), \\(BA = 21 - AC\\), and \\(DE = CE\\), what is the length of \\(BF\\)?\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to determine the lengths of the segments \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), \\(CD\\), \\(DE\\), \\(EF\\), and finally \\(BF\\). Let's start by identifying the given information and performing the necessary calculations step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the given lengths and relationships:**\n   - \\(AC = 15\\)\n   - \\(CE = 21\\)\n   - \\(CF = 27\\)\n   - \\(BA = 21 - AC = 21 - 15 = 6\\",
